From 87b9f396ff05e30890cc6c88e62b01d25f8b9901 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Neil Johnson <njohnson@roku.com>
Date: Wed, 2 Sep 2026 10:32:20 +0100
Subject: [PATCH 08/26] aslink: index symbols by areax so the map is not
 quadratic

lstarea() found the symbols belonging to an area by scanning the
entire symbol hash table once per section of that area - and did
it twice, once to count the symbols and again to collect them.
Map generation therefore cost O(sections * symbols), which is
invisible while a program has a handful of areas and becomes the
dominant cost of linking when it has thousands. Measured on ten
modules of one area per function, with -m:

     2,000 sections    0.32 s
    10,000 sections   10.36 s

against 0.02 s to link the same 10,000 symbols in a single area.
Nine tenths of that ten seconds was map generation, and it is
reached more often than it looks: SDCDBfopen() forces mflag on, so
any link asking for .cdb debug output pays it whether or not a map
was wanted.

Index the symbols once instead. lkasym() in lksym.c walks the hash
table a single time and links each symbol into a list rooted at
the areax that defines it, using new s_alp, a_syp and a_sytp
fields; lstarea() then walks only its own area's symbols. map()
calls it after symdef(), which is where every symbol acquires an
areax.

Symbols are appended to the tail of each list so that walking a
list yields the same order the old hash-table scan did - buckets
ascending, and within a bucket from the head of the chain. That
matters because the sort which follows is stable, so the order
decides how symbols sharing an address are listed.

The sort was a bubble sort over the collected array, which is the
other half of the cost. It is replaced by qsort() over an array of
struct msym, each carrying the symbol, its relocated address and
its collection index. qsort() is not required to be stable, so
msymcmp() falls back on the collection index when addresses are
equal, preserving the previous ordering exactly.

Together:

     2,000 sections    0.32 s  ->  0.03 s
    10,000 sections   10.36 s  ->  0.76 s

Of that 0.76 s only 0.05 s is now map generation; the remaining
0.71 s is the link itself, where lkparea() scans the global area
list once per A record. That is a separate quadratic and is not
touched here.

Verified with a standalone aslink built from linksrc/*.c, compared
against one built from the unmodified sources:

- Default map output is unchanged: four modules of 30 sections each,
  five labels sharing every address to exercise the sort order,
  linked with -m, -m1, -m -w, -m1 -w, -m -d, -m -q and -m -u -j.
  Map, .noi and hex identical apart from the timestamp line.
- Built with -Wall with no new warnings.

